    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d710a-17">

                <pb facs="0267" n="87" xml:id="pg87"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Potsdam/7–1845</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Bohlen
                        Notes</hi><anchor n="2" xml:id="d710a-17-f1"/></head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Potsdam</placeName>, <date
                        ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-chapter-heading" calendar="gregorian"
                        type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1945-07-18T15:04:00+03:00">July
                        18, 1945, 3:04 p.m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#right">3 <hi rendition="#small-caps">p</hi>.</p>
                <p>S:<anchor n="3" xml:id="d710a-17-f2"/> [blank]</p>
                <p>M<anchor n="4" xml:id="d710a-17-f3"/> [blank]</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pres</hi><anchor n="5" xml:id="d710a-17-f4"
                    />—lakes—Black</p>
                <p>S—I must tell you the<del rendition="#strikethrough">y</del>
                    <del rendition="#strikethrough">the</del> news—hands Pres note from Sato &amp; message from the Emperor<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d710a-17-f5"/></p>
                <p>B<anchor n="7" xml:id="d710a-17-f6"/>—I remember that H— H—<anchor n="8"
                        xml:id="d710a-17-f7"/></p>
                <p>S—worthwhile <del rendition="#strikethrough">answer</del> to answer—</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pres</hi> no respect.</p>
                <p>S—not a war—will want lull—might answer as follows no specific—don’t know
                    character—of K<anchor n="9" xml:id="d710a-17-f8"/> Mission—can’t—answer=a
                    refusal</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pres</hi> satisfactory</p>
                <p>M—factual</p>
                <p>B—what [one word illegible] <del rendition="#strikethrough">ins</del> inspired
                    it—fear of what you will do—</p>
                <p>M—I think they guess</p>
                <p>S—Can see our forces tanks etc</p>
                <p>M—not only to USSR</p>
                <p>S—to US. Eng &amp; [one word illegible]</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pres</hi>—we had indicati[on] from Sweden<anchor
                        n="10" xml:id="d710a-17-f9"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d710a-17-f1" n="2" type="source" xml:id="d710a-17-f1-content">
                <p>Transcribed for this volume from longhand notes in pencil. In interviews with a
                    Department of State historian on January 25 and February 8, 1960, Bohlen expressed certainty (since at earlier
                    conferences of Heads of Government it had been his practice, without exception,
                    to transcribe his notes into minutes or memoranda of conversations) that he
                    transcribed these notes into a memorandum of conversation shortly after the
                    meeting took place. Because of Stalin’s
                    reference to the peace feeler from Japan, it is possible that such a memorandum
                    would have been given special security handling, but a search of the
                    Department’s various file collections and of the Truman and Byrnes Papers
                    has failed to bring such a memorandum to light. A literal transcription of the
                    original notes is therefore printed here. For a fuller reconstruction of this
                        Truman–Stalin conversation, prepared by Bohlen in March 1960 on the basis of these notes and from
                    memory, see <ref target="#d1419">document No. 1419</ref>, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">post</hi>.</p>
                <p>For another account of this meeting, see Byrnes, <hi rendition="#italic">Speaking Frankly</hi>, p. 205.
                        Byrnes subsequently (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">All in One Lifetime</hi>, p. 290) and erroneously
                    ascribed the subject matter of this conversation to the Truman–Stalin meeting of July 17. See <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg43">p. 43</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d710a-17-f2" n="3" xml:id="d710a-17-f2-content">
                <p>Stalin.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d710a-17-f3" n="4" xml:id="d710a-17-f3-content">
                <p>Molotov.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d710a-17-f4" n="5" xml:id="d710a-17-f4-content">
                <p>President Truman.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d710a-17-f5" n="6" xml:id="d710a-17-f5-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1945Berlinv01#d586">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>,
                        document No. 586</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d710a-17-f6" n="7" xml:id="d710a-17-f6-content">
                <p>Byrnes.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d710a-17-f7" n="8" xml:id="d710a-17-f7-content">
